# TypeSafeSDK 0.3.0

## Client and questions
{: .col-2}

### Strict semantic API

```elixir
client = TypeSafeSDK.new_client()
questions = [
  urgent: TypeSafeSDK.noul("Urgent?"),
  team: TypeSafeSDK.choice("Team?", billing: "Payments", technical: "Product"),
  severity: TypeSafeSDK.score("Severity?", ["Low", "High"])
]
prepared = TypeSafeSDK.prepare!(questions)
{:ok, response} = TypeSafeSDK.evaluate(client, state, prepared)
```

### Tuple constructors

```elixir
TypeSafeSDK.Question.Noul.new("Urgent?", true: "Time-sensitive")
TypeSafeSDK.Question.Choice.new("Team?", [billing: nil, technical: nil])
TypeSafeSDK.Question.Score.new("Severity?", ["Low", "High"])
```

Each also has `new!`. Legacy `TypeSafeSDK.Noul/Choice/Score.new` still returns
legacy structs; `system_one` still uses string response keys.

## Answers and policy
{: .col-2}

```elixir
team = TypeSafeSDK.Response.fetch!(response, :team)
TypeSafeSDK.Answer.Choice.ranked(team)
TypeSafeSDK.Answer.Choice.margin(team)
TypeSafeSDK.Answer.confidence(team)
TypeSafeSDK.Answer.gate(team, act: 0.9, review: 0.7)
TypeSafeSDK.Answer.yes?(response.answers.urgent, 0.85)
TypeSafeSDK.Answer.Score.expected_level(response.answers.severity)
TypeSafeSDK.Answer.Score.max_level(response.answers.severity)
TypeSafeSDK.Answer.Score.normalized(response.answers.severity)
```

Thresholds are explicit application policy, not safety guarantees. Expected and
modal Score levels differ; Noul confidence is derived as max(p, 1-p).

## Batch, errors and tests
{: .col-2}

```elixir
TypeSafeSDK.evaluate_many(client, states, prepared,
  max_concurrency: 8, max_pending: 32, ordered: true, on_error: :collect,
  task_timeout_ms: 40_000, attempt_timeout_ms: 5_000)
TypeSafeSDK.evaluate_stream(client, states, prepared, ordered: false)
TypeSafeSDK.Error.retryable?(error)
TypeSafeSDK.Error.retry_after(error)

client = TypeSafeSDK.Test.client()
client = TypeSafeSDK.Test.stub(client, urgent: {:noul, 0.9})
# Run application code, then verify before the creating process exits.
TypeSafeSDK.Test.verify!(client)
TypeSafeSDK.Test.close(client)
```

Unordered results have batch_index; errors use details.batch_index. Request
`timeout` and retry policy values still use seconds. Retries can repeat processing.

## Contract and operations tools

```bash
mix typesafe.schema.export
mix typesafe.schema.verify
mix typesafe.capabilities
mix typesafe.record --output tmp/live
```

Recording needs a real API key; no live data is fabricated. Unadvertised transport
bounds remain unverified. Semantic telemetry excludes automatic prompt/state data.


## 0.3 runtime controls
{: .col-2}

```elixir
cancel = Pristine.Cancellation.new()
{:ok, response} = TypeSafeSDK.evaluate(client, state, prepared,
  cancellation: cancel,
  max_request_bytes: 262_144,
  response_contract: [on_unknown_answer: :error])

TypeSafeSDK.Prepared.keys(prepared)
TypeSafeSDK.Prepared.fingerprint(prepared)
TypeSafeSDK.Response.metadata(response)
TypeSafeSDK.RuntimeCapabilities.report(client)
```

Per-call retry keys inherit omitted client retry fields; `retry: false` disables
retry for that call. Cancellation uses Pristine directly and cannot prove that a
remote service did not begin processing a request.
